<article class="env-lemma" id="035J">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/035J">Lemma <span data-tag="035J">29.54.5</span>.</a> Let </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ Y_2 \ar[d]_{f_2} \ar[r] &  Y_1 \ar[d]^{f_1} \\  X_2 \ar[r] &  X_1 }  \]
</div>
  <p> be a commutative diagram of morphisms of schemes. Assume $f_1$, $f_2$ quasi-compact and quasi-separated. Let $f_ i = \nu _ i \circ f_ i'$, $i = 1, 2$ be the canonical factorizations, where $\nu _ i : X_ i' \to X_ i$ is the normalization of $X_ i$ in $Y_ i$. Then there exists a unique arrow $X'_2 \to X'_1$ fitting into a commutative diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ Y_2 \ar[d]_{f_2'} \ar[r] &  Y_1 \ar[d]^{f_1'} \\  X_2' \ar[d]_{\nu _2} \ar[r] &  X_1' \ar[d]^{\nu _1} \\  X_2 \ar[r] &  X_1 }  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemmas <a href="/tag/035I" data-tag="035I">29.54.4</a> (1) and <a href="/tag/01WL" data-tag="01WL">29.45.6</a> the base change $X_2 \times _{X_1} X'_1 \to X_2$ is integral. Note that $f_2$ factors through this morphism. Hence we get a unique morphism $X'_2 \to X_2 \times _{X_1} X'_1$ from Lemma <a href="/tag/035I" data-tag="035I">29.54.4</a> (2). This gives the arrow $X'_2 \to X'_1$ fitting into the commutative diagram and uniqueness follows as well. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>